## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of basic memory management without dynamic allocation, a common pattern in low-latency systems.

## Key Concepts

- Fixed-size pool basics
- Free-list within block memory
- Alignment and safety checks
- Simple double-free detection (debug)

## Code Example

```c
#define BLOCK_SIZE 64
#define N_BLOCKS 1024
typedef struct Block { struct Block* next; unsigned char payload[BLOCK_SIZE - sizeof(struct Block*)]; } Block;
static Block blocks[N_BLOCKS];
static Block* free_list;

void pool_init(void){
  for(int i=0;i<N_BLOCKS-1;i++) blocks[i].next = &blocks[i+1];
  blocks[N_BLOCKS-1].next = NULL;
  free_list = &blocks[0];
}
void* pool_alloc(void){
  if(!free_list) return NULL;
  Block* b = free_list;
  free_list = b->next;
  return (void*)b->payload;
}
void pool_free(void* p){
  Block* b = (Block*)((char*)p - offsetof(Block, payload));
  b->next = free_list;
  free_list = b;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect double-free in a release build?
- How would you extend this to multi-threaded use without locks?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical mastery of low level synchronization, memory ordering, and reusability. A barrier is fundamental in frame sync, parallel tasks, and NUMA awareness, but many candidates overfit to simple mutex patterns.

## Key Concepts

- C11 atomics and memory ordering
- Sense-reversing barrier design
- Reuse safety and race avoidance
- Spin-wait costs and pause hints
- Microbenchmark methodology

## Code Example

```c
#include <stdatomic.h>
#include <sched.h>
volatile atomic_int arrivals;
volatile atomic_int generation;

void barrier(int N){
  int gen = atomic_load_explicit(&generation, memory_order_acquire);
  if (atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&arrivals, 1, memory_order_acq_rel) == N-1){
    atomic_store_explicit(&arrivals, 0, memory_order_release);
    atomic_store_explicit(&generation, 1 - gen, memory_order_release);
  } else {
    while (atomic_load_explicit(&generation, memory_order_acquire) == gen){
      sched_yield();
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for false sharing avoidance across cores?
- How would you measure livelock risk and scale beyond 16 threads?

